comments.body:
[~luke.pearson], I had a look at this issue now I am more familiar with the search_near API since I have been working on WT-7924 with [~jie.chen].

*Context:*

With the new prefix search near feature, in order to have better performance, we want to avoid traversing all keys as we are only interested in the ones which have the prefix we are looking for. This was done as part of WT-7264.

When {{search_near}} is used with {{prefix_key}} enabled, the cursor will be positioned on the first key that matches the prefix we are looking for. However, that key may not be visible, hence we need to look for another key that matches the prefix and is visible.

If we end up being on a key that no longer matches the prefix, we return {{WT_NOTFOUND}} from __cursor_row_next to do an early exit as per the code [here|https://github.com/wiredtiger/wiredtiger/blob/develop/src/btree/bt_curnext.c#L434-L443]: 
{noformat}
        /*
         * If the cursor has prefix search configured we can early exit here if the key that we are
         * visiting is after our prefix.
         */
        if (F_ISSET(&cbt->iface, WT_CURSTD_PREFIX_SEARCH) && prefix != NULL &&
          __wt_prefix_match(prefix, &cbt->iface.key) < 0) {
            /* It is not okay for the user to have a custom collator. */
            WT_ASSERT(session, CUR2BT(cbt)->collator == NULL);
            WT_STAT_CONN_DATA_INCR(session, cursor_search_near_prefix_fast_paths);
            return (WT_NOTFOUND); {noformat}
However, the issue described in this ticket arises when the cursor is positioned on the last key of a page. We will return {{WT_NOTFOUND}} from __cursor_row_next and this will prevent us from continuing the search even though there might be visible keys with the same prefix on the next page. This condition is checked [here|https://github.com/wiredtiger/wiredtiger/blob/develop/src/btree/bt_curnext.c#L412-L414]:
{noformat}
        /* Check for the end of the page. */
        if (cbt->row_iteration_slot >= page->entries * 2 + 1)
            return (WT_NOTFOUND); {noformat}
Finally, when {{WT_NOTFOUND}} is returned from the function __cursor_row_next, we exit the function __wt_btcur_next_prefix as per the code [here|https://github.com/wiredtiger/wiredtiger/blob/develop/src/btree/bt_curnext.c#L763-L764]: 
{noformat}
                /*
                 * We can directly return WT_NOTFOUND here as the caller expects the cursor to be
                 * positioned when traversing keys for prefix search near.
                 */
                if (ret == WT_NOTFOUND && F_ISSET(&cbt->iface, WT_CURSTD_PREFIX_SEARCH))
                    return (WT_NOTFOUND);{noformat}
To summarise, {{search_near}} with {{prefix_key}} enabled may end up with {{WT_NOTFOUND}} for two reasons (maybe more ?):
 * The prefix of the current key no longer matches the one we are looking for (valid reason to do an early exit).
 * We are at the end of the page (reason why this ticket was created).

*Suggested fix:* 

Instead of adding another WT error code, my suggestion would be to double-check why we are getting {{WT_NOTFOUND}} from __cursor_row_next. If we get {{WT_NOTFOUND}} but the prefix still matches, we shall continue:

 
{noformat}
diff --git a/src/btree/bt_curnext.c b/src/btree/bt_curnext.c
index 8506083e0..077ba6bdf 100644
--- a/src/btree/bt_curnext.c
+++ b/src/btree/bt_curnext.c
@@ -760,7 +760,8 @@ __wt_btcur_next_prefix(WT_CURSOR_BTREE *cbt, WT_ITEM *prefix, bool truncating)
                  * We can directly return WT_NOTFOUND here as the caller expects the cursor to be
                  * positioned when traversing keys for prefix search near.
                  */
-                if (ret == WT_NOTFOUND && F_ISSET(&cbt->iface, WT_CURSTD_PREFIX_SEARCH))
+                if (ret == WT_NOTFOUND && F_ISSET(&cbt->iface, WT_CURSTD_PREFIX_SEARCH) &&
+                  __wt_prefix_match(prefix, &cbt->iface.key) != 0)
                     return (WT_NOTFOUND);
                 break; {noformat}
With this fix, your reproducer works OK and I am attaching another reproducer I used, very similar to yours that does not reproduce the issue either with the suggested fix.

*Questions:*

After this analysis, I have a couple of questions.

Question 1

In the case of a {{search_near}} with {{prefix_key}} enabled, if the first key on which our cursor is positioned is not an exact match but is visible, do we still need to look for other keys? I think we should simply return that key. In the case of a {{search_near}} *without* {{prefix_key}} enabled, it makes sense to look forward and backward due to visibility issues and knowing that no prefixes are considered.

Question 2

Due to the prefix matching, it is not possible that search_near sets cmp to -1 since the prefix would not match, is that correct? If I am correct, I don't think anything related to prefix matching should appear in [bt_curprev.c|https://github.com/wiredtiger/wiredtiger/blob/develop/src/btree/bt_curprev.c]. If I am missing something, then the fixes suggested above should be applied to that file too.

Thanks for your answers [~luke.pearson]. The special handling in {{search_near}} could be simple even though I would need to do more testing, but it would look like this:
{noformat}
--- a/src/btree/bt_cursor.c
+++ b/src/btree/bt_cursor.c
@@ -733,6 +733,10 @@ __wt_btcur_search_near(WT_CURSOR_BTREE *cbt, int *exactp)
                 goto done;
         }
 
+        /* It is not necessary to go backwards when search_near is used with a prefix. */
+        if (F_ISSET(cursor, WT_CURSTD_PREFIX_SEARCH))
+            goto done;
+
         /*
          * We walked to the end of the tree without finding a match. Walk backwards instead.
          */ {noformat}
If we don't go backwards, this would reduce the number of keys traversed by two. I would not expect the numbers of traversed keys to be N*2 but N with N being the number of keys with the same prefix.
